   $q$The Latin root struct means "build," the prefix de- means "undo or reverse," and -tion makes a noun. What does "destruction" mean in the last paragraph?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The act of planning a bridge before building it","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-root-clues"},{"text":"The act of being torn apart until nothing stands","is_correct":true},{"text":"The act of testing how much weight a bridge holds","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"context-only-guess"},{"text":"The act of building something from many pieces","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-prefix"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The act of being torn apart until nothing stands$q$, $q$De- reverses struct, so destruction is the undoing of something built, and only "a pile of sticks remained." Without the prefix, the word would be construction, which means building.$q$, 'draft', '3438700d-14ca-5161-9d81-0dc771d0b28d')
