-- Passage rg6-authors-purpose-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('78dae235-b4aa-589d-adfe-8e66f54dd7a7', 'rg6-authors-purpose-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How a Glacier Shapes a Valley$q$, $q$A glacier is a huge mass of ice that moves slowly over land. It forms in places where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer. Year after year, the snow piles up and presses down on the layers beneath it. Over time, the lower layers turn into thick, solid ice.

When a glacier becomes heavy enough, gravity pulls it downhill. It may travel only a few inches or a few feet each day. Even so, the moving ice is powerful. As it slides, it freezes onto loose rocks and drags them along. These rocks act like sandpaper, scraping the ground beneath the glacier.

Rivers usually carve valleys shaped like the letter V. A glacier works differently. Because the ice fills the whole valley, it grinds away the sides as well as the bottom. After the glacier melts, it leaves behind a wide valley shaped more like the letter U.

Glaciers also leave clues about where they have been. Long scratches on bare rock show the direction the ice moved. Piles of rock and gravel, called moraines, mark the places where a glacier stopped and dropped its load.

The next time you see a photograph of a broad mountain valley with steep walls, look closely. You may be looking at the work of ice that moved slowly, but never stopped, for thousands of years.$q$, 'draft')
