  ('2234fb3b-82f5-5601-a260-551485595f23', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-authors-purpose'), null, 'hard',
   $q$In the last paragraph, the author contrasts "great discoveries made in a single moment" with knowledge "built slowly." What point is the author making?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Weather is easier to study today because satellites work in a single moment.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Slow, ordinary work deserves credit, even though it is less often celebrated.","is_correct":true},{"text":"History books should stop writing about famous scientists and inventors.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"exaggerates-position"},{"text":"Discoveries made in a single moment are usually wrong and should be ignored.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"exaggerates-position"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Slow, ordinary work deserves credit, even though it is less often celebrated.$q$, $q$The author says the volunteers "remind us" that important knowledge is built slowly by people rarely named, so such work deserves recognition. The author does not say quick discoveries are wrong or should be ignored.$q$, 'draft', '649e7613-7c4a-5146-9677-91d7765e8225')
