-- Passage rg6-authors-purpose-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b88db7c0-80f6-59a6-b769-ab4b78cfad92', 'rg6-authors-purpose-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Paper Pages or Screens?$q$, $q$Many students now do some of their reading on tablets or other screens, while others prefer printed books. Researchers and teachers have studied both, and each choice has strengths.

Screens offer convenience. A single tablet can hold hundreds of books, which saves space and weight in a backpack. Readers can make the text larger, look up an unfamiliar word with a tap, or read in a dim room without a lamp. For students who have trouble seeing small print, these features can make reading much easier.

Printed books have advantages of their own. Some studies suggest that readers remember details from long passages slightly better on paper. One possible reason is that a physical book gives clues about place. A reader may recall that an important scene happened near the bottom of a left-hand page, about halfway through the book. Paper also has no alerts or games to pull a reader's attention away.

The best choice may depend on the task. A quick article or a book read for fun might work well on a screen. A difficult chapter for science class, which a student needs to study closely, might be easier to understand in print.

Instead of asking which format is better, readers can ask a more useful question: what am I reading, and why? The answer can help each person pick the tool that fits.$q$, 'draft')
