-- Passage rg6-character-setting-plot-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c43be36e-ca27-5185-ae5f-c332705ab248', 'rg6-character-setting-plot-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Number, Please$q$, $q$In the summer of 1915, the town of Clover Bend had exactly one telephone switchboard, and it sat in the front room of the Pruitt family's house. Mrs. Pruitt was the operator. When anyone in town picked up a telephone, a little flap dropped on her board. Then she plugged in a cord and asked, "Number, please?"

On the morning of the county fair, Mrs. Pruitt had to drive her prize pies to the fairgrounds. She left her twelve-year-old daughter, Opal, in charge of the board. Opal had watched her mother for years, but she had never run it alone.

At first, the calls came slowly. Then, around ten o'clock, flaps began dropping faster than Opal could plug in cords. A prize calf had wandered away from the fair, and half the town was calling to report it. One farmer had seen it by the creek. Another had seen it near the schoolhouse.

Opal's hands shook. She connected two callers to the wrong houses and had to apologize. Then she took a breath and grabbed a pencil. Instead of rushing, she wrote each caller's report on a slip of paper with the time beside it.

When the livestock judge finally called the switchboard himself, Opal was ready. "It was by the creek at ten after ten," she said, "and near the schoolhouse at ten thirty. It's heading toward the fairgrounds."

The calf was found an hour later, grazing behind the pie tent. That evening, Mrs. Pruitt found the stack of paper slips beside the board. She read every one. Then she asked Opal to take the board every Saturday for the rest of the summer.$q$, 'draft')
