-- Passage rg6-character-setting-plot-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('70c0b6ab-5e5a-5005-88a8-25764561e46e', 'rg6-character-setting-plot-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Lead-Off$q$, $q$Tomas Villanueva had swum the anchor leg of the relay all season. The anchor swam last, and the anchor touched the wall when the race was won. Tomas loved the roar of the crowd in those final seconds.

At practice before the league championship, Coach Haruna posted the new lineup on the pool door. Tomas read it twice. He was swimming first, the lead-off leg. Darnell Price, who had joined the team in January, was now the anchor.

Tomas pushed through the locker room door so hard that it banged against the wall. During warm-ups he barely spoke to Darnell. When the coach asked if he had any questions, he only shook his head.

That night, he watched the video of their last meet on his tablet. He timed every swimmer's leg. Darnell's final lap was the fastest on the team. Tomas's start, the dive and the first fifteen meters underwater, was the fastest by far. The coach hadn't punished him. She had put each of them where he was strongest.

On Saturday, the pool building was loud and smelled of chlorine. Tomas crouched on the starting block. At the horn, he exploded off the edge and came up a full body length ahead. He touched the wall and turned to watch.

The lead held through the second and third legs. On the last lap, a swimmer from Eastview pulled even with Darnell, and Tomas found himself shouting Darnell's name louder than anyone. Darnell touched first by a fingertip.

Afterward, Tomas walked over and held out his hand. "Next season," he said, "teach me how you finish like that."$q$, 'draft')
