-- Passage rg6-evidence-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b0775be7-e56f-55a8-8abf-783dd7a4fc1a', 'rg6-evidence-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Rolling on the Court$q$, $q$On Saturday mornings, the gym at Ridgeview Recreation Center fills with the squeak of rubber tires. The Ridgeview Rollers, a youth wheelchair basketball team, practice there every week. Some players use wheelchairs in daily life. Others do not, but they join because they enjoy the game.

Wheelchair basketball follows most of the same rules as the standing game. The hoop is the same height, and the court is the same size. A few rules are different, however. A player may push the wheels twice while holding the ball, but then must pass, shoot or bounce it. Players who push three times are called for traveling.

The chairs used on the court are not ordinary wheelchairs. Their wheels tilt outward at the bottom, which makes the chair harder to tip over during quick turns. A small wheel at the back keeps players from falling backward. Coach Teresa Okafor says new players are often surprised by how light the chairs are.

The team's practices focus on skills that standing players may never think about. Players learn to stop by grabbing the wheels and to spin in tight circles. They practice shooting from a seated position, which means the power must come from the arms and shoulders rather than the legs. Okafor reminds them that a strong shot from the chair takes months of practice.

Last season, the Rollers won only two games. This year they have already won five, and attendance at home games has doubled. Several players credit the extra practice time the center added in the fall.$q$, 'draft')
