-- Passage rg6-figurative-language-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('92e5ae9d-69d4-57d8-bd18-4c93d44f09b4', 'rg6-figurative-language-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Rivers of Ice$q$, $q$A glacier looks like a frozen giant sleeping on a mountainside. It seems perfectly still. In fact, a glacier is always moving, though usually too slowly for anyone to notice.

Glaciers form in places where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer. Year after year, the snow piles up. The weight of the new layers presses down on the old ones and squeezes them into solid ice. This process can take hundreds of years.

Once the ice becomes thick and heavy enough, gravity begins to pull it downhill. The glacier creeps forward a few centimeters or a few meters each day. As it travels, it acts like a giant sheet of sandpaper. Rocks frozen into its bottom scrape the ground beneath it, carving deep valleys and polishing stone smooth.

Scientists study glaciers carefully because they are sensitive to changes in temperature. When summers grow warmer, a glacier may shrink, or retreat, faster than new snow can replace it. Photographs taken decades apart sometimes show a glacier that has pulled back like a turtle into its shell.

Glaciers also store a large share of the world's fresh water. In many regions, melting glacier ice feeds the rivers that farms and towns depend on during dry months. For those communities, a healthy glacier is a savings account of water, slowly paid out over time.$q$, 'draft')
