  ('9513abb8-c153-5954-b676-1f64a684297b', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$In paragraph 5, the author calls a healthy glacier "a savings account of water, slowly paid out over time." What does this metaphor mean?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Towns must pay money to scientists to use water that comes from the glacier.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"The glacier keeps its water locked away so that no one can ever use it.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-extreme"},{"text":"The glacier loses all of its water at once whenever summer weather begins.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"The glacier stores water and releases it little by little when people need it.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The glacier stores water and releases it little by little when people need it.$q$, $q$A savings account holds money that is taken out a bit at a time, just as the glacier holds water and feeds rivers "during dry months." The word "slowly" rules out the idea that it releases everything at once.$q$, 'draft', '92e5ae9d-69d4-57d8-bd18-4c93d44f09b4')
