-- Passage rg6-figurative-language-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1f1ed3c4-411a-5394-b541-ca3671e3ad70', 'rg6-figurative-language-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Messages on the Wing$q$, $q$Long before phones and email, people sent important messages with the help of birds. Homing pigeons have an amazing talent. If you carry one far from its home, it will find its way back, often across hundreds of kilometers.

For thousands of years, people took advantage of this skill. Sailors and traders carried pigeons on long journeys. When they needed to send news home, they tied a tiny note to a bird's leg and released it. The pigeon would shoot toward home like an arrow.

Pigeon messengers were especially useful because they were fast and dependable. A bird could cover in one day a distance that might take a rider on horseback much longer. Newspapers once used pigeons to deliver news of faraway events. Fishermen used them to announce their catch before the boats reached shore.

How do pigeons find their way? Scientists are still piecing together the puzzle. Pigeons seem to use the position of the sun, and they may sense Earth's magnetic field. Some researchers think pigeons also remember landmarks such as rivers and roads, reading the land below like a map.

Today, people rarely need pigeons to carry news. Yet many hobbyists still raise and race them. When a pigeon flutters down onto its home loft after a long flight, its owner greets it like a returning hero.$q$, 'draft')
