insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9d831aac-925a-566a-85a5-cecaadd5b6c0', 'rg6-figurative-language-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Behind the Button$q$, $q$Playing a video game can feel effortless. You press a button, and a character leaps across the screen. Behind that simple jump, however, stands a whole team of people and months or even years of work.

Most games begin as a spark of an idea. A designer might imagine a puzzle about guiding water through pipes, or a story about a lost cat finding its way home. The team then builds a rough version called a prototype. It is plain and often messy, but it lets them test whether the idea is fun.

Programmers are the builders of the game world. They write code, the instructions that tell the computer what to do. Artists create the characters, backgrounds, and colors. Sound designers add music and effects, such as the crunch of footsteps on gravel.

Then comes testing. Testers play the game again and again, hunting for bugs, which are mistakes in the code. A single bug can make a character fall through the floor or freeze the whole screen. Fixing bugs is a bit like weeding a garden. As soon as you pull one, another seems to pop up.

Finally, after countless changes, the game is ready to launch. For the team, release day is a mountaintop. From there, they can finally look back at the long climb that brought them to the top.$q$, 'draft')
